, the square root is less than a negative number $(-4),$ so no number that we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to the given inequality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-115044" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b42339288b320302545090fab399ed4f5d3556ce3e95bc1508c6271b13a469f2"} {"question" "q-115044" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-115044" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<-\\frac{8}{7}"] ["math" "x<4"] ["math" "x>-4"] ["math" "x>\\frac{8}{7}"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-115044" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No solution"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $6-2\\sqrt{x-7}\\le 14.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-2\\sqrt{x-7} & \\le 14 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{x-7} & \\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-7} & \\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-4),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx-7\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge 7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b42339288b320302545090fab399ed4f5d3556ce3e95bc1508c6271b13a469f2"} {"question" "q-276739" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276739" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ge"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276739" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276739" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-278280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $3+2\\sqrt{4x+1}>7.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-278280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2\\sqrt{4x+1} & >7 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{4x+1} & >4 \\\\ \\sqrt{4x+1} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4x+1})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ 4x+1 & >4 \\\\ 4x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\frac{3}{4},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{1}{4},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )\\cap [-\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )=(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $\\frac{3}{4}<x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-278280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b42339288b320302545090fab399ed4f5d3556ce3e95bc1508c6271b13a469f2"} {"question" "q-278280" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-278280" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-278280" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-278280" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1\\ge -5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1 & \\ge -5 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3x+1} & \\ge -6 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+1} & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(3).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x+1})}^{2} & \\le {3}^{2} \\\\ 3x+1 & \\le 9 \\\\ 3x & \\le 8 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{8}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}]\\cap [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )=[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{8}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b42339288b320302545090fab399ed4f5d3556ce3e95bc1508c6271b13a469f2"} {"question" "q-87508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{8}{3}"] ["math" "x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{8}{3}"] ["text" "$x\\le -\\frac{1}{3}$ or $x\\le \\frac{8}{3}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{8}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $12-5\\sqrt{3x+2}\\le 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-5\\sqrt{3x+2} & \\le 2 \\\\ -5\\sqrt{3x+2} & \\le -10 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+2} & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x+2})}^{2} & \\ge {2}^{2} \\\\ 3x+2 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 2 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -2 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{2}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )\\cap [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )=[\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b42339288b320302545090fab399ed4f5d3556ce3e95bc1508c6271b13a469f2"} {"question" "q-87782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "x\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{2}{3}"] ["text" "$x\\le -\\frac{2}{3}$   or  $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $2\\sqrt{4x+3}-13\\le -5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{4x+3}-13 & \\le -5 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{4x+3} & \\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{4x+3} & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(4).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4x+3})}^{2} & \\le {4}^{2} \\\\ 4x+3 & \\le 16 \\\\ 4x & \\le 13 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -3 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{13}{4},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{13}{4}].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{3}{4},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{13}{4}]\\cap [-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )=[-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{13}{4}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{13}{4}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b42339288b320302545090fab399ed4f5d3556ce3e95bc1508c6271b13a469f2"} {"question" "q-87781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}"] ["math" "x\\ge -\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{13}{4}"] ["text" "$x\\le -\\frac{3}{4}$ or $x\\ge \\frac{13}{4}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-87781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{4+5x}+6\\ge 2.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{4+5x}+6 & \\ge 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{4+5x} & \\ge -4 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+5x} & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(12).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4+5x})}^{2} & \\le {12}^{2} \\\\ 4+5x & \\le 144 \\\\ 5x & \\le 140 \\\\ x & \\le 28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+5x & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5x & \\ge -4 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 28,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,28].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{4}{5},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{4}{5},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,28]\\cap [-\\frac{4}{5},\\infty )=[-\\frac{4}{5},28]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{4}{5},28],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{4}{5}\\le x\\le 28.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b42339288b320302545090fab399ed4f5d3556ce3e95bc1508c6271b13a469f2"} {"question" "q-276744" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276744" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276744" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276744" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\le"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276744" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "q-276744" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "28"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/state.json" "file_sha256" "654c86ef142201420cad09bff278f0f49779a39bf69c2afe7b10c27541fde326"} {"question" "e-7654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{x+3} + 1 \\geq  0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/example-7654.json" "file_sha256" "d6d1427c09526faebabc94c7a9c0a0e6eb359f4c62da4dbe3174aade922e37de"} {"question" "e-7654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+3}+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+3} & \\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{x+3})}^{3} & \\ge {(-1)}^{3} \\\\ x+3 & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/example-7654.json" "file_sha256" "d6d1427c09526faebabc94c7a9c0a0e6eb359f4c62da4dbe3174aade922e37de"} {"question" "e-7655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $3\\sqrt{x-2} +7 \\geq 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/example-7655.json" "file_sha256" "8066a9bfef57c51649e3dab7d96f4d8508b4bc9f20e0f05fb9943d46403ed4d8"} {"question" "e-7655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{x-2}+7 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\nx-2 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\geq 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/example-7655.json" "file_sha256" "8066a9bfef57c51649e3dab7d96f4d8508b4bc9f20e0f05fb9943d46403ed4d8"} {"question" "e-7656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $7-\\sqrt{x+3} < 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/example-7656.json" "file_sha256" "dc6b4cb1c1fbbe96332b320ac8be3a543b96de9224603b4060e8dc9eaed80355"} {"question" "e-7656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt{x+3} & <5 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x+3} & <-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+3})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ x+3 & >4 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x > 1,$ which can be expressed as $\\left( 1,\\infty \\right).$ The domain of the square root is $x \\geq -3,$ which can be expressed as $\\left[ -3,\\infty \\right).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\left( 1,\\infty \\right) \\cap \\left[ -3,\\infty \\right)  =\\left( 1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in \\left( 1,\\infty\\right),$ which can also be written as $x > 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981580/example-7656.json" "file_sha256" "dc6b4cb1c1fbbe96332b320ac8be3a543b96de9224603b4060e8dc9eaed80355"}] "usage" {"q-87508" [] "e-7654" [] "e-7656" [] "e-7655" []} "basis" 892 "retractions" [[17592186157832 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-2\\sqrt{3x + 1} + 1 \\ge - 5$."] [17592186157065 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{x+3} + 1 \\geq 0$."] [17592186157065 :question/worked-solution "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[3]{x + 3} + 1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x + 3} & \\ge - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[3]{x + 3})^{3} & \\ge (-1)^{3} \\\\ x + 3 & \\ge - 1 \\\\ x & \\ge - 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186157066 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3\\sqrt{x-2} +7 \\geq 4$."] [17592186157066 :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{x - 2} + 7 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge - 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1)$, so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\nx-2 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2\n$$"] [17592186157067 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $7-\\sqrt{x+3} < 5$."] [17592186157067 :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 - \\sqrt{x + 3} & < 5 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x + 3} & < - 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x + 3} & > 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2)$. So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt{x + 3})^{2} & > 2^{2} \\\\ x + 3 & > 4 \\\\ x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x > 1$, which can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nThe domain of the square root is\n\n$$\nx \\geq -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $[MATH: \\left[-3,\\infty \\right).]$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$[MATH: \\left(1,\\infty \\right) \\cap \\left[-3,\\infty \\right) =\\left(1,\\infty\\right)]$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in \\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as $x > 1$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186156245 17592186156246 17592186156247 17592186156248 17592186156249] "immutable_fields" [[17592186156244 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156244 :answer-field/id] [17592186156244 :answer-field/key] [17592186156244 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
